  "query_preview": "..\nThe overall weather pattern over the Southwestern US continues\nto be driven by a 500mb upper low centered off the Southern CA\ncoast. A few afternoon thunderstorms formed yesterday with the \npresence of this upper low, but has since quieted down during\nthe overnight period. Blended Total Precipitable Water\nsatellite data suggests PW in the neighborhood of 1\", good for \n150-175% of normal for this time of year. \n\nSigns continue to point to a more active Wednesday afternoon\nrelative to Tuesday. This is due to the 500mb low drifting\neastward toward southeast CA. Supported by the left exit region\nof a passing upper jet streak and an upper trough axis, high\nresolution models are showing the most likely area of\nthunderstorm activity extending from Tucson south and east\nWednesday afternoon and evening. Another area of activity looks\nto be over the White Mountains. Thermodynamic profiles are\nimpressive, with forecast CAPE above 1500 J/kg and downdraft\nCAPE upwards of 2000 J/kg. With these indices in mind, gusty \noutflow winds and resulting blowing dust appear to be the \ngreatest threats. Indeed, HREF and REFS ensembles show 10-25% \nchance of wind gusts >50 kts in portions of far southeast AZ and\neastward Wednesday evening. We also can't rule out slow moving \nthunderstorms causing isolated very heavy rainfall; WPC has \nissued a marginal risk for flash flooding in our region.\n\nThursday may be the last day in this round of convective\nactivity. While the 500mb low remains close in proximity to the\nCA/AZ border, the upper jet streak drifts northeastward in the\nnext 24 hours, also pushing the area of upper diffluence in the\nsame direction. As such, the best chance of thunderstorms on\nThursday will remain over the White Mountains. By Friday, the\nupper low will phase in with the Western US longwave trough and\neject northward. The result will be drier weather with broad\nwesterly flow. Look for temperatures to remain within degrees of\nnormal through the weekend, then trend warmer as high pressure\nbuilds into the region next week.",
